1
00:00:01,020 --> 00:00:05,123
In this video, I'm going to talk about
concurrency and race conditions.

2
00:00:05,123 --> 00:00:09,857
So we're going to understand concurrency
concerns, in terms of Linux kernel and

3
00:00:09,857 --> 00:00:12,740
the driver that we're going to write.

4
00:00:12,740 --> 00:00:16,161
We're going to understand what race
conditions are and how to avoid them.

5
00:00:17,540 --> 00:00:20,090
So a little bit about kernel drivers and
concurrency.

6
00:00:20,090 --> 00:00:23,708
Your book talks about concurrency bugs
as being the easiest to create and

7
00:00:23,708 --> 00:00:25,160
some of the hardest to find.

8
00:00:25,160 --> 00:00:27,340
Probably not a good combination, right?

9
00:00:27,340 --> 00:00:32,769
And it mentions that these are made
ubiquitous, the concurrency bugs,

10
00:00:32,769 --> 00:00:38,390
that is are made ubiquitous by symmetric
multiprocessing or SMP systems.

11
00:00:38,390 --> 00:00:44,259
So the advent of SMP has made it more
easy to create concurrency bugs and

12
00:00:44,259 --> 00:00:49,550
has made these even more difficult
to root out when they occur.

13
00:00:49,550 --> 00:00:53,493
So this is all something that we have
to live with as a fact of dealing with

14
00:00:53,493 --> 00:00:57,740
hardware that's more capable of
running Linux in the first place.

15
00:00:57,740 --> 00:01:02,159
So one thing worth discussing probably
that the book doesn't really go into

16
00:01:02,159 --> 00:01:04,852
detail is the difference between SMP and
AMP,

17
00:01:04,852 --> 00:01:07,769
symmetric versus asymmetric
multiprocessing.

18
00:01:07,769 --> 00:01:12,095
So when we say symmetric multiprocessing,
what we mean is that there

19
00:01:12,095 --> 00:01:16,869
are multiple CPUs that are fundamentally
time sharing across the same RAM or

20
00:01:16,869 --> 00:01:18,599
just sharing the same RAM.

21
00:01:18,599 --> 00:01:23,668
As opposed to asymmetric multiprocessing
where you have multiple CPUs but

22
00:01:23,668 --> 00:01:28,840
they have their own block of memory
that they're each working with.

23
00:01:28,840 --> 00:01:32,619
So we could simplify the concurrency
issues by just having dedicated RAM for

24
00:01:32,619 --> 00:01:33,158
each CPU.

25
00:01:33,158 --> 00:01:36,941
But it turns out it's a lot harder to
write an operating system that way, and

26
00:01:36,941 --> 00:01:39,021
AMP is generally not supported by Linux.

27
00:01:39,021 --> 00:01:43,575
So we're going to have to deal with SMP
and one of the fallout of that is that we

28
00:01:43,575 --> 00:01:47,700
have to be really careful and
cognizant about concurrency books.

29
00:01:50,540 --> 00:01:55,643
So the book gives you an example of
how you could create a concurrency bug

30
00:01:55,643 --> 00:02:00,747
given the skull driver example,
if you were to not implement locking,

31
00:02:00,747 --> 00:02:05,940
it was giving an example about
where a currency bug could occur.

32
00:02:05,940 --> 00:02:11,767
So it shows this example of some code
that is a part of a handling for,

33
00:02:11,767 --> 00:02:14,851
I believe it's a right procedure.

34
00:02:16,340 --> 00:02:21,076
And what you see here, if you look at this
code is that the code has decided that it

35
00:02:21,076 --> 00:02:22,860
needs to kmalloc new buffer.

36
00:02:22,860 --> 00:02:26,672
So it's looking at the data pointer
deciding that the fact that it's NULL

37
00:02:26,672 --> 00:02:30,240
means that there is some data
that needs to be malloc.

38
00:02:30,240 --> 00:02:34,025
And the book is talking about what would
happen if you didn't have appropriate

39
00:02:34,025 --> 00:02:37,640
locking and you could end up with
a situation where two different processes

40
00:02:37,640 --> 00:02:39,840
could access at the same time.

41
00:02:39,840 --> 00:02:43,704
So if you think through what could happen
in this case, when the first process hits

42
00:02:43,704 --> 00:02:46,911
the kmalloc, we'll talk about this
in a little more detail later.

43
00:02:46,911 --> 00:02:51,237
But it turns out there's a decent chance
that the tasks switch could occur during

44
00:02:51,237 --> 00:02:52,080
that kmalloc.

45
00:02:52,080 --> 00:02:55,044
So perhaps while
the kmalloc is in process,

46
00:02:55,044 --> 00:02:59,783
there's a task switch that occurs
[COUGH] and a second process begins.

47
00:02:59,783 --> 00:03:05,222
And so while the second process happens to
go through this exact same block of code,

48
00:03:05,222 --> 00:03:10,212
dptr data is still NULL, so we start
with the kmalloc on the second process.

49
00:03:12,451 --> 00:03:16,155
And now we have a task switch again and
this time during the task switch,

50
00:03:16,155 --> 00:03:17,776
the first process completes.

51
00:03:17,776 --> 00:03:21,324
And when kmalloc completes,
we write dptr data and

52
00:03:21,324 --> 00:03:24,551
we finish go through
the rest of the function.

53
00:03:25,840 --> 00:03:28,576
And then after the first
process completes,

54
00:03:28,576 --> 00:03:32,320
the second process would complete,
do the same thing again,

55
00:03:32,320 --> 00:03:37,040
go back through the rest of
the function and reassigned dptr data.

56
00:03:37,040 --> 00:03:40,113
So what ends up being stored
in dptr data in this scenario,

57
00:03:40,113 --> 00:03:42,507
is the output of the second process,
right?

58
00:03:42,507 --> 00:03:47,009
Because the first process wrote it and
then even though we had just completed

59
00:03:47,009 --> 00:03:51,387
this check for dptr NULL in the second
process and it was originally NULL.

60
00:03:51,387 --> 00:03:54,751
By the time we completed,
it wasn't null anymore, but

61
00:03:54,751 --> 00:03:57,920
we didn't check it again and
so we just overrode it.

62
00:03:57,920 --> 00:03:59,307
And so, what does that mean?

63
00:03:59,307 --> 00:04:03,412
It means that the dptr data values written
with the kmalloc from the second process

64
00:04:03,412 --> 00:04:07,240
and the kmalloc from the first process,
that pointer is essentially lost.

65
00:04:07,240 --> 00:04:11,042
So if later in the code
we're freeing that data,

66
00:04:11,042 --> 00:04:16,900
we're going to not free the memory that
we allocated from the first process.

67
00:04:16,900 --> 00:04:21,672
So this would be an example of how you
could end up with a memory leak due

68
00:04:21,672 --> 00:04:25,061
to race condition based on lock or
lack thereof.

69
00:04:26,240 --> 00:04:31,138
So you might say, well that seems really
unlikely that these two processes could

70
00:04:31,138 --> 00:04:33,320
just hit at exactly the wrong time.

71
00:04:33,320 --> 00:04:37,400
And if that other process was delayed just
a little bit and we would have waited for

72
00:04:37,400 --> 00:04:38,900
the dptr data to get written,

73
00:04:38,900 --> 00:04:43,117
then we wouldn't have ended up with this
kind of race condition in the first place.

74
00:04:43,117 --> 00:04:46,640
So maybe I can just ignore it because
it's going to be really unlikely.

75
00:04:46,640 --> 00:04:50,806
Well, the book makes the point that
if you're clocking processors at

76
00:04:50,806 --> 00:04:53,983
the gigahertz type speed
that clocked at these days,

77
00:04:53,983 --> 00:04:57,370
one-in a million events can
happen every few seconds.

78
00:04:57,370 --> 00:05:01,750
And so the other thing to point out is
that the likelihood that it could happen

79
00:05:01,750 --> 00:05:06,335
could be dependent on a whole bunch of
different factors that you have no control

80
00:05:06,335 --> 00:05:09,911
over and that might change in
ways that you wouldn't expect.

81
00:05:09,911 --> 00:05:14,894
And so especially it could be that
you'd never hit this in five years of

82
00:05:14,894 --> 00:05:19,455
operation and then suddenly you
get some new part that seems to be

83
00:05:19,455 --> 00:05:24,357
completely unrelated that's updated
in your build environment and

84
00:05:24,357 --> 00:05:29,120
suddenly this happens once a day,
rather than never in ten years.

85
00:05:29,120 --> 00:05:34,297
So these kind of problems can just
appear in seemingly random times and

86
00:05:34,297 --> 00:05:38,959
then can be really confusing in
terms of what their source was.

87
00:05:38,959 --> 00:05:43,056
And then this last one, which is a variant
of Murphy's law that I'm going to call

88
00:05:43,056 --> 00:05:46,846
Dan's law, which is that it's usually
a race condition is going to happen

89
00:05:46,846 --> 00:05:50,288
the first time your boss or
a customer tries to run your software.

90
00:05:50,288 --> 00:05:53,994
So it'll work great on your bench and
then that once in a million event's

91
00:05:53,994 --> 00:05:59,040
going to happen the first time, it's
really important that it wouldn't happen.

92
00:05:59,040 --> 00:06:01,272
So we need to plan for
these ahead of time and

93
00:06:01,272 --> 00:06:05,251
we can't just use the let's ignore it
because it's really unlikely approach.

94
00:06:07,040 --> 00:06:12,018
So the sources of concurrency issues, will
have multiple user space processes running

95
00:06:12,018 --> 00:06:16,471
that could be interacting with us and
they could be running on different CPUs.

96
00:06:16,471 --> 00:06:20,738
And SMP systems are going to be executing
your code simultaneously on different

97
00:06:20,738 --> 00:06:21,531
processors.

98
00:06:21,531 --> 00:06:25,688
So because the processors are running
asynchronous with each other,

99
00:06:25,688 --> 00:06:30,561
it'll be possible for you to enter the
same code path in two different processes.

100
00:06:32,040 --> 00:06:34,171
Because kernel code is preemptable,

101
00:06:34,171 --> 00:06:37,050
the driver code can lose
the processor at anytime.

102
00:06:37,050 --> 00:06:40,455
An interrupt could happen
anytime in your code and

103
00:06:40,455 --> 00:06:44,680
you could end up at at any
instruction that's not protected.

104
00:06:44,680 --> 00:06:47,142
You could end up getting interrupted and

105
00:06:47,142 --> 00:06:50,118
having the same kind of
concurrency bugs occur.

106
00:06:50,118 --> 00:06:54,119
And device interrupts are going to be
completely asynchronous, there's nothing

107
00:06:54,119 --> 00:06:57,661
you can do to control, Windows
are going to happen relative to your code.

108
00:06:58,740 --> 00:07:00,534
And also when you're
working with hardware,

109
00:07:00,534 --> 00:07:03,810
you have to consider that the device could
disappear while you're working with it.

110
00:07:03,810 --> 00:07:07,073
So you can't assume that because
the device was available when you started

111
00:07:07,073 --> 00:07:10,457
a function, it might not be there by
the time you actually tried to access it.

112
00:07:14,751 --> 00:07:18,261
So some tips for avoiding race conditions.

113
00:07:19,740 --> 00:07:22,466
The easiest thing to do is just
to avoid shared resources,

114
00:07:22,466 --> 00:07:25,371
whenever it's possible to do that,
that's one way to do it.

115
00:07:25,371 --> 00:07:29,825
So one thing that the book suggests is
you can avoid global variables, however,

116
00:07:29,825 --> 00:07:31,659
if you have no global variables,

117
00:07:31,659 --> 00:07:35,340
would that mean that you
wouldn't have shared resources?

118
00:07:35,340 --> 00:07:39,212
And the answer of course is no,
because referencing a global variable or

119
00:07:39,212 --> 00:07:43,593
referencing a pointer that you're passing
into functions is really essentially

120
00:07:43,593 --> 00:07:44,493
the same thing.

121
00:07:44,493 --> 00:07:47,965
You're still referencing
the same block of memory and so

122
00:07:47,965 --> 00:07:52,028
you still have those same kind of
sharing and concurrency issues.

123
00:07:54,940 --> 00:07:58,942
So in order to decide if you need
to manage access through locking or

124
00:07:58,942 --> 00:08:01,840
if you could have a concurrency problem.

125
00:08:01,840 --> 00:08:04,269
What you should think
about is is hardware or

126
00:08:04,269 --> 00:08:07,770
another resource shared beyond
a single thread of execution?

127
00:08:07,770 --> 00:08:11,975
So is it possible that more than
one thread could could execute for

128
00:08:11,975 --> 00:08:15,040
this particular code I'm writing?

129
00:08:15,040 --> 00:08:19,330
And then is it possible that one of the
threads could encounter an inconsistent

130
00:08:19,330 --> 00:08:23,945
view of the resource, meaning, could I be
part of the way through making some change

131
00:08:23,945 --> 00:08:27,140
when another thread tries
to access the resource?

132
00:08:27,140 --> 00:08:29,590
And if the answer the most
of those questions are yes,

133
00:08:29,590 --> 00:08:32,599
then that means that it's up to
you to explicitly manage access.

134
00:08:32,599 --> 00:08:37,927
And the way we'll manage access is through
either locking or mutual exclusion.

135
00:08:41,240 --> 00:08:46,172
So one point the book makes is
that you need to be careful about

136
00:08:46,172 --> 00:08:51,007
when you notify the kernel and
your requirements in terms of

137
00:08:51,007 --> 00:08:55,861
the lifetime of the function
after you notify the Colonel.

138
00:08:55,861 --> 00:08:58,972
So if you are making an object
available to the kernel,

139
00:08:58,972 --> 00:09:01,630
you need to make sure
that it's initialized.

140
00:09:01,630 --> 00:09:04,934
And if you are still using the object, for

141
00:09:04,934 --> 00:09:09,940
the lifetime of the object as long
as the kernel knows about it,

142
00:09:09,940 --> 00:09:14,690
you need to make sure that
the resource remains available.

143
00:09:14,690 --> 00:09:17,591
So this is especially important
when it comes to locking.

144
00:09:17,591 --> 00:09:20,631
Because we need to make sure
that locks are initialized for

145
00:09:20,631 --> 00:09:22,989
any objects that we provide to the kernel,
so

146
00:09:22,989 --> 00:09:27,161
that there will be a correct state of the
lock before we try to access the object.